  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1131 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nAn extremely quiet weather pattern is expected to continue for\nthe next several days. Tonight, a sfc trof axis will eventually\nfragment and then move east during the morning hours. As this\ndoes, expect light southwesterly winds ahead of the boundary to\nbecome light northerly in its wake. Lows tonight will be on\nthe mild and above freezing side. Expected upper 30s across\nwestern Kansas to lower 40s across south-central Kansas. \n\nTuesday will continue to feature uneventful weather. A light\nnortherly wind will continue during the day. Highs tomorrow will\nbe in the mid to upper 60s. No major fire weather concerns are\nexpected tomorrow, even with the above normal temperatures.\nAfternoon relative humidity levels and winds will just be too\nmarginal for any major fire weather concerns. Another night of\nmild lows are expected for Wednesday morning, with values only\nbottoming out mainly in the 40s. \n\nMore tranquil weather is expected midweek. Kansas will be under\nsplit flow aloft, which favors no major synoptic systems moving\nthrough. The net result is a dry forecast and continued above\nnormal highs and lows. Highs should peak in the mid to upper 60s\nwith lows in the 40s Wednesday. Again, even with the warmth...\nno major fire weather concerns are expected with weak winds and\nmarginal afternoon relative humidity levels.\n\nThere is going to be a sharper 850-hPa temperature gradient\nThursday. Cooler air will exist across central Kansas and even\nfarther to the NE. A wider range of highs are expected across\nsouthwest Kansas with mid 50s at Hays to mid 60s at Elkhart.\nBoth winds and relative humidity levels looks far too marginal\nfor significant fire weather impacts.\n\nLooking at Friday and into the weekend... the long range models\nand their ensemble counterparts are indicating mid level ridging\nFriday and into the weekend. This would support the notion of a\ndry forecast and with a continue of above normal highs and lows.\nThe GEFS and ENS are indicating for the potential in a weather\npattern change in the Nov 15-19th range. There is considerable\nspread in the ensemble QPF members. It also seems the GEFS and\nmore conservative with a progressive, light hitting system than\ncompared to the ENS. Something to watch. Confidence is just too\nlow that far out in time.",
